welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Jonathan Watson an assistant  professor of sociology and international  affairs at Yale University professor  Watson is a comparative historical  sociologist with teaching and research  interests in north african society and  politics he works on the areas of state  formation colonialism and empire  ethnicity and nationalism urban and  rural contentious politics and Islamic  social movements today we'll talk with  Professor Woodson about his new book  constructing Morocco colonial state  building and the struggle to define the  nation welcome professor Watson Thank  You Allen let's begin with an overview  of your book and the question at its  foundation okay this book looks at a big  question in in examining how nations are  formed how nations are defined and I'm  taking a very specific case I look at  Morocco to analyze how this plays out in  an empirical sense and the critical  question that I'm interested in is in  its you know in the colonial period in  Morocco which was between 1912 and 1956  you had an outside power European  actually the French and the Spanish that  come in to Morocco ostensibly to do  state building to do state formation and  what I do is look at this period and  analyze these two processes state  formation or state building on the one  side and then nation building or nation  formation on the other and what I'm  interested in is the specific reasons  for how and why Moroccan national  identity was defined the way that it was  which in the end and not independence in  1956 you have a very strong definition  of an Arab identity Muslim identity and  then the critical importance of the king  as a figurehead that unifies the nation  okay and what was the inspiration for  writing the book this book the origins  of this go back to a time when I was  when I first was working in Morocco  several years ago when you drive around  the country you see  from the north to the south of the  country on hills or mountain sides three  words that are painted on to the rock so  you can see from the highway at the top  you have Allah God and then at the  bottom you have a lot on the nation and  L Melek the king and you have this  triptych these three words God nation  and king that basically define what  Moroccan national identity or at least  the official version of that is and when  I with this project what is interested  in is going back into the story the  genealogy of how this particular  definition of Moroccan identity came to  be okay I know you're in the final  stages of actually writing the book but  tell us a little bit about the  methodology how are you doing the  research well to uncover the way that  the story came came about and basically  there's an assumption here that this is  a process rather than a given moroccan  identity is not something that just  exists but that it's something has  historically evolved and has been  constructed over time and what i was  interested in is getting into the the  various players that were involved in  that story and by examining the colonial  period i basically looked at examining  the from on the state building side the  french colonial administration then you  have an urban nationalist movement that  gets mobilized in the 1930s the the king  who plays an important role and then i  was really interested in several other  groups that are the non elites but that  played an important role that has not  been the story on their side has not  been told as extensively and these were  the tribal groups that were outside of  the the urban areas which primarily  berber-speaking the country had a very  sizable Jewish minority and then the  other kind of underlying a group that  doesn't get a lot of coverage in the  history of this period are women and all  the various roles that they played and  so in the methodology that I pursued was  to go back into colonial archives to  identify other kind of non-traditional  types of sources to get some of these  voices particularly the tribal Berber  side and women to bring their  perspective on the process of nation  formation that went on during the  colonial periods  okay let's talk a little bit more  specifically about each of those groups  the women the Berbers and the Jews how  did each define help to define Moroccan  identity during this period the that  process actually works out in tandem  with that the other major players that  are involved which and this is the story  that usually gets told is the way that  the colonial administration sought to  prevent the unification of a Moroccan  resistance and then used policies to  divide the Moroccan society or to  reinforce divisions that they perceive  that were there and what they actually  tried to do was to use ethnicity to try  to divide between Arab and Berber to use  religion to divide between Jews and  Muslims and then use gender also there's  a fundamental division that was in place  in terms of education you have voice in  one school girls in another of course  law was very much gender based in which  men have different rights than women do  and so to start with the Berbers this is  these populations which lived in more  inaccessible areas in the mountains for  instance that are off of the coast and  in the deserts they were actually the  the most intractable groups that the  French had the most difficulty in  Spanish also in pacifying or militarily  conquering these areas and so you  actually have several decades it's not  until the mid 1930s that this process of  pacification is completed and the the  Berber is that resistance and then the  kind of subsequent story where they get  incorporated into the colonial army it  is a whole part of Moroccan history that  has not been well chronicled and  actually a really critical element in in  how both the nationalists and the French  were trying to control definitions of  the nation and on the French side  they're basically trying to say that the  Arabs and the Berbers were completely  distinct that the Berbers weren't really  Muslim and on the other side the Arabs  were basically trying to say the  opposite that there was a fundamental  unity there of Arabs and Muslims so to  examine that the Berber perspective what  I did is look at a tree an archive of  poetry that was actually gathered during  that time period which is really a  unique way to get a voice  from a population that didn't have  written sources and in this oral of  medium you get to hear their  perspectives on on the conquest on this  dilemma of this almost apocalyptic sense  of impending doom as the French are  conquering the country and you actually  get to hear women and men so it was  fascinating that's a fascinating aspect  that I was able to uncover in the study  so I'm sure this is actually oral people  recited the poetry - or is it it's not  written this so in its original  incarnation these were poems that were  were chanted in a public ceremony in you  know in a song but during the period of  the colonial period as the past this  military conquest was happening there  was a French officer whose hobby was to  gather all of this poetry he sat down  and transcribed that and then he had  multiple moroccan assistants  interlocutors that went out into these  areas and gathered this from basically  from the north of the country to the  south and so it provides us really  amazing insight into how these group you  know insurgent groups what the  resistance fighters you know depending  on your perspective their side to the  story what conclusions do you reach in  the book I know it's not finished yet  but right whether in terms of the the  basic question and this is a you know  relevant question in light of what's  happening in the American involvement in  Iraq for instance or in Afghanistan in  these these places where you there's a  lot of talk about failed States or you  know how do you actually go about state  formation nation formation you know to  go back into this period where you had a  somewhat similar circumstance with an  outside power that's interested in these  processes and in some ways controlling  them I think one of the conclusions one  of the basic conclusions is that that  political context of this outside power  coming in does really impact the the  process of identity formation but impact  us in ways that are really difficult to  to pre-ordained or to actually control  and so like for in the Moroccan case you  actually had a very interactive  processes where the inputs from the  colonial regime from an urban  nationalist group from a traditional  government the Sultan the King that they  kept in place and then from these other  outlier groups all of this goes into a  very complex process that works over  time  and in the other major conclusion is  that that process doesn't stop at a  given point that this is a continuing  process okay and what was the most  surprising thing you found in doing the  research for the book the the I think  that stuff that was most fascinated by  and that was most surprising was to get  into the story that I was talking about  before of this berber side to Morocco's  colonial history which the you know the  official nationalist versions that come  out after independence definitely  incorporate these and basically label  them as a part of a teleological  seamless process of the Moroccan nation  emerging but what you see what in  getting into that Pro tree itself you  see lots of competing voices trying to  understand what is happening in in  processing as a communal level at this  greater level beyond a tribal  identification in some sense it's kind  of a pre national sense of Moroccan  identity but in a very different angle  than the urban Arab nationalist were  were defining that and what's  interesting there is kind of the  intimacy of some of the poetry that  deals with this conquest at the level of  their families being being killed at the  same time that they're also processing  this at a very corporate level thanks  very much for being here with us today  and sharing some of your research thank  you you're welcome  for more information about professor  watson and his work please visit our  website at yale.edu backslash macmillan  report be sure to join us again for  another episode of the macmillan report  made possible through funding from the  whitney and betty macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale
 

